in @glass
in @magic/distinction
in @mirror/store

# Property: a shard whose Spencer-Brown mark reads "load-bearing" MUST
# live under `shards/`, not `boot/std/`. This property names the rule
# that drives the WHOLE-SHARD `boot/std/*` → `shards/*` migration via
# the kintsugi loop, at the altitude the sibling per-declaration
# `@epistemologic/pact/substrate_source_in_shards` cannot reach.
#
# THE DECLARATIVE HALF of the self-migration bilateral (recognition
# #53 THIRD instance — the load-bearing-at-family-root instance).
# The operational half landed at
# `@kintsugi/fracture/relocate` (Mara `5c12808`, PREREQ-2, Tick 3 per
# the canonical spec §8 landing sequence); this shard names the pact
# whose `failure(opacity_map)` verdict the operational half consumes.
# Per Mara §4 of the canonical spec, per Taut §2 of the boundary
# scout (@knife → @magic/distinction substrate-already-had-the-word),
# per Seam §Q4 Phase D ratification (the bilateral is substrate-honest
# generalisation, not invention).
#
# === The load-bearing distinction ===
#
# Not every shard under `boot/std/` needs to move. The sibling pact
# `@epistemologic/pact/substrate_source_in_shards` names the
# per-declaration migration rule ("every declaration lives in
# shards/") — succeeds vacuously for shards with no consumers, but
# the corpus-organisation altitude question is different: WHICH
# `boot/std/*` shards are load-bearing enough that leaving them at
# legacy altitude carries real cost?
#
# The Spencer-Brown mark (`@magic/distinction`) partitions the
# `boot/std/` corpus into two states via one drawn boundary:
#
#   - marked_state   = load-bearing. Real `in @<path>` consumers
#                      elsewhere in shards/; the shard's OID appears
#                      in some downstream shard's `impacted_by`
#                      closure. Migration surfaces as opacity.
#   - unmarked_state = residual. No shards/ consumers cross the
#                      shard's OID; the shard is legacy scaffolding
#                      the substrate can leave in place per Mara §7
#                      S5 (residual-set discipline). No opacity.
#
# The mark rides `@magic/distinction`'s `distinction_well_formed`
# predicate for Spencer-Brown well-formedness (condensation +
# cancellation axioms). The pact composes:
#
#   surface_as_mark(shard.location) = mark
#   mechanism_as_distinction_space(shard.impacted_by_closure)
#                                  = distinction_space
#
# The mark is well-formed iff the load-bearing/residual partition is
# non-degenerate at this shard — a shard with impacted_by closure
# strictly containing shards/ OIDs is load-bearing; a shard whose
# closure is empty or contains only boot/std/ OIDs is residual.
#
# === Substrate-pull discipline ===
#
# Per `@glass`, `transparency` is the verdict carrier and `opacity`
# names one located fact. Per `@magic/distinction`, the mark is the
# substrate's carrier for the load-bearing/residual partition —
# @knife was NOT invented (Taut scout §2). Per `@mirror/store`,
# `impacted_by(oid) -> [oid]` names the reverse-closure the mark
# reads to distinguish load-bearing from residual. Substrate had
# every carrier before this pact landed; this pact NAMES the
# composition.
#
# === Bilateral shape ===
#
# Bilateral with `@kintsugi/fracture/relocate` (the operational
# half; Mara `5c12808`). Same shape as prior instances:
#
#   1st  `keyword_matches_depth` + `@kintsugi/fracture/keyword`
#   2nd  `gate_matches_diff_closure` + `@kintsugi/fracture/gate`
#   3rd  `is_load_bearing_in_std` + `@kintsugi/fracture/relocate`
#
# THIRD INSTANCE lifts the pattern to LOAD-BEARING at family-root
# altitude: the prior instances rewrite ONE SITE per opacity; this
# instance MIGRATES A WHOLE SHARD per opacity, with cross-corpus
# ancestry propagation via `impacted_by(shard.oid)`. Per Seam §Q1,
# the generalisation is substrate-honest — the fracture body reads
# `impacted_by` at family-root, no new machinery invented.
#
# === Verdict surface ===
#
# Verdict surface is transparency<p>:
#   success              -- every load-bearing shard lives under
#                          shards/. No load-bearing shards remain at
#                          boot/std/ altitude. Residual shards MAY
#                          remain at boot/std/ (they are NOT
#                          load-bearing; no opacity).
#   failure(opacity_map) -- one or more load-bearing shards live
#                          under `boot/std/`; each opacity names ONE
#                          offending shard (its OID + legacy path)
#                          plus its impacted_by closure — the
#                          ancestry-propagation payload the
#                          operational half consumes.
#   partial(opacity_map) -- mixed corpus state — some load-bearing
#                          shards migrated, some pending. Typically
#                          mid-cascade.
#
# Located transparency (not @glass.verdict): the kintsugi loop
# consumes the opacity_map to direct migration; each opacity names
# ONE shard and the operational half emits ONE morphism (a
# whole-shard splinter naming shards/ destination + N ancestry
# rewrites). The property names the rule; the fracture realises it;
# the kintsugi loop is the bridge.
#
# === Composition with sibling pact ===
#
# `substrate_source_in_shards` (per-declaration; landed 2026-06-17)
# is a LOWER BOUND: it surfaces every boot/std declaration
# uniformly, load-bearing or not. This pact (whole-shard; load-
# bearing filtered) is the UPPER BOUND that drives the actual
# migration order — load-bearing shards migrate FIRST because their
# opacity carries the ancestry-propagation cost; residuals stay
# until the corpus is otherwise clean. The two pacts compose:
# `substrate_source_in_shards` names WHAT could migrate; this pact
# names WHAT MUST migrate to unblock the corpus.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   mark-of-distinction    Spencer-Brown 1969 Laws of Form ch.1; the
#                          load-bearing/residual partition IS a mark.
#   reverse-dependency     Karen Spärck Jones 1972 inverted index /
#                          Bazel `rdeps`; the substrate reads
#                          `impacted_by(oid)` for the filter.
#
# The substrate's contribution is *typing* the load-bearing filter at
# the pact altitude so the kintsugi loop reads it. No invention.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/substrate-self-migration-via-spectral-
#               typing.md §4 (Mara e764a32);
#               docs/scouts/2026-07-10-taut-self-migration-substrate-
#               boundary-scout.md §2 (Taut 8a3b0a4);
#               docs/audits/2026-07-10-seam-phase-d-self-migration-
#               ratification.md §Q4 (Seam 2b56977).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-property-fracture-bilateral]]
#                       (THIRD instance — keyword, gate, relocate),
#                      [[architecture-kintsugi-bias-lift]],
#                      [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]].
# Related shards: kintsugi/fracture/relocate.mirror (operational half;
#                 consumes the opacity_map this pact emits),
#                 epistemologic/pact/substrate_source_in_shards.mirror
#                 (per-declaration lower bound this pact refines),
#                 epistemologic/pact/gate_matches_diff_closure.mirror
#                 (bilateral SECOND instance),
#                 epistemologic/pact/keyword_matches_depth.mirror
#                 (bilateral FIRST instance),
#                 magic/distinction.mirror (Spencer-Brown adapter),
#                 mirror/store.mirror (impacted_by reverse-closure).
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/epistemologic/pact/is_load_bearing_in_std.mirror` declares
# `@epistemologic/pact/is_load_bearing_in_std` per
# `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace`.
# Keyword/depth property: this declaration is depth-2 from the
# @epistemologic family root; per recognition #46 it uses `pact`.

pact @epistemologic/pact/is_load_bearing_in_std {
  # The impacted_by closure at the shard's OID — the set of
  # downstream OIDs whose forward closure crosses the argument
  # shard. The load-bearing filter reads this closure: non-empty
  # containing shards/-rooted OIDs => load-bearing; empty or
  # containing only boot/std/-rooted OIDs => residual.
  #
  # Composes through `@mirror/store`'s `impacted_by(oid) -> [oid]`
  # at family-root altitude; no new machinery invented per the
  # substrate-already-had-the-word discipline.
  impacted_closure(shard: ref) -> [ref] { \ }

  # The load-bearing mark at the shard. Reads
  # `surface_as_mark(shard.location)` and
  # `mechanism_as_distinction_space(impacted_closure(shard))` from
  # `@magic/distinction`; the Spencer-Brown mark is well-formed
  # iff the shard's impacted_closure contains at least one
  # shards/-rooted OID.
  #
  # Verdict:
  #   marked_state    -- load-bearing. Migration is warranted.
  #   unmarked_state  -- residual. Migration is NOT warranted;
  #                      the shard stays at boot/std/ per Mara §7
  #                      S5 residual-set discipline.
  load_bearing_mark(shard: ref) -> ref { \ }

  # The combined property: `is_load_bearing_in_std(shard)` is
  # failure iff:
  #   (a) `load_bearing_mark(shard)` reads marked_state
  #       (the shard IS load-bearing), AND
  #   (b) shard's source path is rooted at `boot/std/`
  #       (the shard is NOT yet at substrate-honest altitude).
  #
  # Both conditions together are the migration precondition; the
  # `failure(opacity_map)` verdict surfaces the shard AND its
  # impacted_closure so the operational half (`@kintsugi/fracture/
  # relocate`) can emit the whole-shard morphism with correct
  # ancestry propagation.
  #
  # Verdict surface is transparency<p>:
  #   success              -- shard is either residual (unmarked)
  #                          or already at shards/ altitude. Fine.
  #   failure(opacity_map) -- shard is load-bearing AND at
  #                          boot/std/; opacity names the shard's
  #                          OID + legacy path + impacted_closure.
  #   partial(opacity_map) -- multiple shards under evaluation;
  #                          some fail, some pass (typical
  #                          mid-cascade corpus verdict).
  is_load_bearing_in_std(shard: ref) -> transparency { \ }
}

out impacted_closure
out load_bearing_mark
out is_load_bearing_in_std
out @epistemologic/pact/is_load_bearing_in_std
